<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:ti="using:TablerIcons">

  <!--
    The icon set, as one rule rather than as a decision per glyph.

    Every icon in this app is a Tabler outline icon, drawn from the TablerIcons.Avalonia
    package: the web frontend takes the same set from @tabler/icons-react and the GTK grid
    from vendored Tabler SVGs, so a glyph means the same thing on all three surfaces and no
    surface has its own private drawing of a tick (docs/design-language.md).

    The window controls are the one exception, and they are drawn at the bottom of this file.
    They are not this app's icons: they stand in for the caption buttons Windows hid when the
    client area was extended over them, so they are Windows' own shapes at Windows' own size,
    and a reader compares them with the buttons on every other window on the screen rather
    than with the tick beside them.

    Emoji and box-drawing characters are not an alternative. The platform emoji font paints
    in colour and ignores the brush, and a font's box and arrow metrics move with the face,
    so a glyph that has to sit on an optical centre cannot be text.

    TablerIcon is a Control and not a TemplatedControl, so it has no Foreground to inherit
    and its Brush defaults to black. The default below is what keeps that from ever reaching
    a screen; a view that wants another role states it, the same way it states one on a
    TextBlock.
  -->
  <Style Selector="ti|TablerIcon">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="{DynamicResource IconSizeControl}" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="{DynamicResource IconSizeControl}" />
    <Setter Property="StrokeWidth" Value="{DynamicResource IconStroke}" />
    <Setter Property="Brush" Value="{DynamicResource GlyphBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

  <!-- Inside a badge or a pill, where the shape around it is already 15-16px. -->
  <Style Selector="ti|TablerIcon.badge">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="{DynamicResource IconSizeBadge}" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="{DynamicResource IconSizeBadge}" />
  </Style>

  <!--
    The window controls' glyphs. Paths and not text: the codepoints Windows draws these with
    live in Segoe Fluent Icons' private use area, so a face that is missing (an older
    Windows, or any other platform this shell is built for) paints a tofu box in the corner
    of the title bar rather than a close button.

    Drawn rather than taken from the icon set, and the numbers are the platform's: a 10px
    box, a 1px line, mitred corners, no rounding. The Tabler minus, square and cross that
    were here before were the same three lines the rest of the app is drawn with, which is
    exactly what made them wrong - a caption button is read against the other windows on the
    screen, not against the app underneath it.

    Stretch is None so the geometry in TitleBarView maps one unit to one pixel, which is what
    lets its half-pixel coordinates put a 1px line on a whole pixel row.

    The brush is the default only; the buttons override it so the glyph follows their states.
  -->
  <Style Selector="Path.caption">
    <Setter Property="Width" Value="{DynamicResource CaptionGlyphSize}" />
    <Setter Property="Height" Value="{DynamicResource CaptionGlyphSize}" />
    <Setter Property="Stretch" Value="None" />
    <Setter Property="StrokeThickness" Value="{DynamicResource CaptionStroke}" />
    <Setter Property="StrokeJoin" Value="Miter" />
    <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="{DynamicResource GlyphBrush}" />
    <Setter Property="HorizontalAlignment" Value="Center" />
    <Setter Property="VerticalAlignment" Value="Center" />
  </Style>

</Styles>
